\f0\fs40 \cf2 Hot compresses and lid massage are used to help treat an internal hordeolum, also called a stye. This is a small, localized infection of one of the oil glands within the eyelid. It often causes tenderness, swelling, and a firm lump in the lid. These typically improve with conservative treatment over several days.\
\
Heat should be as warm as can comfortably be tolerated (test first to avoid burning the skin). A clean heat mask is often the easiest option, as it retains heat well unlike a cloth. Apply it over closed eyes for about 10 minutes. The goal is to increase blood flow and help the gland drain more easily.\
\
After heating, gentle massage can help encourage drainage. Use a clean fingertip or cotton tip to put gentle pressure on the tender area, and roll toward the lashes. Use light pressure only. The goal is to encourage natural drainage, not to squeeze or force the area.\
\
This treatment is typically done 2 to 4 times per day while the stye is active. Improvement is usually gradual over several days, although some may take longer to fully resolve.\
\
Important tips:\
